+<section id="unrecognized-members">
+<h1>Unrecognized Members</h1>
+<p>The AI Catalog top-level object, Catalog Entry, Host Info, and Publisher
+objects are open: in addition to the members this specification defines,
+they MAY contain members it does not define. Such members are <em>unrecognized members</em>.</p>
+<p>This specification assigns no meaning to any unrecognized member. It takes
+no position on any convention a producer follows when choosing them,
+including conventions defined by other specifications. A producer may use
+unrecognized members to follow such a convention, to annotate a private
+catalog, or to experiment with a candidate field before proposing it.</p>
+<section id="consumer-requirements">
+<h2>Consumer Requirements</h2>
+<p>Consumers:</p>
+<ul>
+<li>MUST NOT reject a document because it contains unrecognized members.</li>
+<li>MUST NOT derive semantics defined by this specification from an
+  unrecognized member. An unrecognized member MUST NOT alter how a consumer
+  interprets <code>identifier</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>url</code>, <code>data</code>, <code>version</code>, or any other
+  defined member.</li>
+<li>SHOULD preserve unrecognized members, unchanged, when republishing or
+  re-serializing a catalog or entry, for example when a catalog operator
+  incorporates an entry from another catalog. Preservation is best effort:
+  an implementation bound to a fixed data model, such as a strongly typed
+  SDK, MAY drop members it cannot represent.</li>
+<li>MUST retain unrecognized members when constructing a signed payload, as
+  required by
+  <a href="#signed-payload-and-jws-construction">Signed Payload and JWS Construction</a>.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Conformance tooling MAY report unrecognized members as informational
+output, for example to surface a misspelled member name, but MUST NOT treat
+their presence as a conformance failure.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="no-stability-guarantee">
+<h2>No Stability Guarantee</h2>
+<p>Any member name this specification does not define MAY be defined by a
+future version, with any meaning. A producer that relies on an unrecognized
+member accepts that risk, and this specification does not coordinate with
+any outside convention before defining a member. Producers that want a
+stable, publisher-owned place for custom data SHOULD use
+<a href="#extensions"><code>extensions</code></a>, whose namespaced keys cannot collide with
+members this specification defines later.</p>
+<p>A future major version MAY narrow or close these objects, depending on how
+the ecosystem uses them.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="trust-and-signing">
+<h2>Trust and Signing</h2>
+<p>Unrecognized members within a Trust Manifest are signed as part of that
+Trust Manifest (see
+<a href="#signed-payload-and-jws-construction">Signed Payload and JWS Construction</a>).
+Unrecognized members anywhere within a catalog are signed as part of that
+catalog's signature (see
+<a href="#catalog-snapshot-coverage">Catalog Snapshot Coverage</a>), subject to
+<a href="#catalog-authorization">Catalog Authorization</a>. In both cases the
+signature authenticates that the signer published those bytes, not what
+they mean.</p>
+</section>
+<section id="relationship-to-extensions">
+<h2>Relationship to `extensions`</h2>
+<table>
+<thead>
+<tr>
+<th></th>
+<th><code>extensions</code></th>
+<th>Unrecognized members</th>
+</tr>
+</thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr>
+<td><strong>Defined by this specification</strong></td>
+<td>Yes: key rules and official extensions</td>
+<td>No</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><strong>Can collide with a future core member</strong></td>
+<td>No, keys are namespaced</td>
+<td>Yes, no stability guarantee</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><strong>Meaning to a consumer of this specification</strong></td>
+<td>Whatever the extension defines</td>
+<td>None</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><strong>Handling</strong></td>
+<td>Ignore unknown keys; retain in signed payloads</td>
+<td>Ignore; preserve on republication; retain in signed payloads</td>
+</tr>
+<tr>
+<td><strong>Intended for</strong></td>
+<td>Publisher-owned, named extensions</td>
+<td>Outside conventions, local annotations, experimentation</td>
+</tr>
+</tbody>
+</table>
+</section>
+<section id="examples">
+<h2>Examples</h2>
+<p>These examples are illustrative. None of them is a convention this
+specification defines or recommends.</p>
+<p><strong>Local annotations in a private catalog.</strong> An internal catalog that is
+never federated may annotate entries with members meaningful only inside the
+organization. These are bare names, so they carry the
+<a href="#no-stability-guarantee">no stability guarantee</a> above; an organization that
+wants protection against future collisions uses <code>extensions</code> instead.</p>
+<pre><code class="language-json">{
+  &quot;identifier&quot;: &quot;urn:air:example.com:hr:benefits-agent&quot;,
+  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;application/a2a-agent-card+json&quot;,
+  &quot;url&quot;: &quot;https://agents.example.com/benefits/.well-known/agent-card.json&quot;,
+  &quot;costCenter&quot;: &quot;CC-4471&quot;,
+  &quot;approvedBy&quot;: &quot;platform-security&quot;,
+  &quot;reviewDate&quot;: &quot;2026-09-30&quot;
+}
+</code></pre>
+<p><strong>Prototyping a candidate field.</strong> An implementer can ship a field that is
+under discussion for a future version, gather experience, and bring that
+experience to the proposal. Until the field is defined, consumers of this
+specification ignore it.</p>
+<pre><code class="language-json">{
+  &quot;identifier&quot;: &quot;urn:air:example.com:weather&quot;,
+  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;application/mcp-server-card+json&quot;,
+  &quot;url&quot;: &quot;https://api.example.com/mcp/weather.json&quot;,
+  &quot;lifecycle&quot;: &quot;deprecated&quot;,
+  &quot;supersededBy&quot;: &quot;urn:air:example.com:weather-v2&quot;
+}
+</code></pre>
+</section>
+</section>
